; kloop's own section, appended when [sandbox] allow_network is OFF: "no
; network" should not also mean "no local sockets". Go's httptest binds a
; loopback port for nearly every HTTP test, and the blanket deny turned that
; into `listen tcp6 [::1]:0: bind: operation not permitted` — which made the
; model escalate out of the sandbox and stay out for the rest of the session,
; so every later command lost the auto-allow that containment buys. Upstream
; codex emits the same three rules (sandboxing/src/seatbelt.rs), in its
; proxy-restricted mode.
;
; What these filters do, measured on macOS 25.4 rather than assumed:
;
;   - `host` accepts only `*` and `localhost`. `(local ip "127.0.0.1:*")` is not
;     a narrower rule, it is a profile syntax error: "host must be * or
;     localhost in network address".
;   - On the REMOTE address the filter discriminates: outbound to anything off
;     this machine stays denied (checked against 1.1.1.1:443).
;   - On the LOCAL address it does NOT: `(local ip "localhost:*")` admits a
;     `listen` on `0.0.0.0` exactly as `*:*` would. It is spelled `localhost`
;     here to state the intent — if macOS ever teaches the local filter to
;     discriminate, this tightens on its own — but today it is not a guarantee,
;     and the boundary below is what actually holds.
;   - `listen()` is gated by network-inbound, not network-bind: drop the inbound
;     rule and a bound socket still cannot listen, which is the httptest case
;     itself. So there is no spelling of this that admits httptest and keeps
;     listening sockets on loopback only.
;
; The boundary, then: nothing leaves the machine, but a sandboxed process can
; listen on every interface rather than just loopback. That is seatbelt's
; ceiling, not an oversight — accepted deliberately (plan 170).
(allow network-bind (local ip "localhost:*"))
(allow network-inbound (local ip "localhost:*"))
(allow network-outbound (remote ip "localhost:*"))
